<h1 class="article-title">Karosta Canal Remediation Project Continues</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>By the end of April this year, the Liepāja SEZ Authority plans to announce a procurement procedure for remediation works in Karosta Canal to remove historical pollution. It is continuing the project “Remediation of the Historically Contaminated Site in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja, Phase II”, aimed at reducing the negative impact of historically contaminated sediments on the environment, human health and the ecological condition of the Baltic Sea.</p><p>For many years, Karosta served as a base for the Soviet Baltic Fleet, including ships and submarines. Naval activities discharged untreated wastewater, petroleum products and heavy metals, including mercury and lead, into the canal. As a result, Karosta Canal became one of the most polluted sites in Latvia.</p><p>During the first phase of the Karosta Canal remediation project, approximately 50,000 m³ of large-scale waste was removed from the water. In Phase II, the plan is to dredge approximately 200,000 m³ of sediments contaminated with petroleum products and heavy metals, cleaning an area of 36 hectares. After several studies, the Liepāja SEZ Authority concluded that the contaminated sediment layer in some parts of the canal exceeds 1.5 metres. Five of the most polluted locations have been identified for remediation.</p><p>Ivo Koliņš, Head of the Market Research and Analytics Department at the Liepāja SEZ Authority, explains that remediation will focus on the commercially used part of the canal. “The middle of the canal, where vessels move and propellers cause turbulence, can stir up these materials. When we receive reports of an oil film appearing in the canal, in 90% of cases it is historical pollution.”</p><p>To achieve the project’s objective, remediation works are expected to remove 202,247 m³ of sediments from the canal bed that do not meet statutory standards and contain 400 tonnes of hazardous chemicals. The project is funded under the Norwegian Financial Mechanism 2014–2021 programme “Climate Change Mitigation, Adaptation and Environment”. Total project costs are EUR 5,117,647.05, of which EUR 4,350,000.00 comes from the Norwegian Financial Mechanism.</p><p> </p><p>Additional information:</p><p>Between 1994 and 2010, the Liepāja SEZ Authority and government institutions financed dozens of geoecological studies to prepare the Karosta Canal remediation project. The decision to implement Phase I was made only in 2012. With funding from the EU Cohesion Fund, the project “Remediation of the Historically Contaminated Site in Karosta Canal, Port of Liepāja, Phase I” was implemented by 31 December 2015. Large-scale man-made waste, including scrap metal, concrete blocks and timber, was recovered and disposed of from an area of 78 hectares. Approximately 60,000 m³ of contaminated sediment was also removed from the most polluted 10-hectare section and deposited in a specially equipped disposal site at the eastern end of Karosta Canal.</p><p>The project implementation period is from 1 August 2021 to 31 December 2023.</p><p>The project budget is EUR 5,117,647.05, including EUR 4,350,000.00 in co-financing from the Norwegian Financial Mechanism.</p><p> </p><p>“Working together for a green Europe”</p></div>
